\begin{abstract}
We develop, from first principles and in full generality, the discrete Fourier transform on an
arbitrary finite abelian group $G$, viewed as the decomposition of the regular representation into
characters. Starting from the two orthogonality relations for characters we prove Fourier
inversion, Parseval's identity, the convolution theorem, and the identity
$\F^{2} = |G|\cdot\mathcal{R}$ where $\mathcal{R}$ is the reflection $f \mapsto f(-\,\cdot\,)$; we
show that the transform is precisely the Wedderburn decomposition $\C[G] \cong \C^{\dualG}$ of the
group algebra, whence $\C[G]$ is reduced. We prove the Donoho--Stark uncertainty principle
$|\supp f|\cdot|\supp \hat f| \ge |G|$ for every $f \neq 0$, and exhibit an explicit family of
extremals: all nonzero multiples of modulated translates of subgroup indicators,
$f(x) = c\,\chi(x)\,1_{H}(x-a)$. Along the way we obtain the subgroup--annihilator duality
$|H|\,|H^{\perp}| = |G|$ \emph{from Plancherel's identity} rather than from Pontryagin duality of
the quotient, together with a Poisson summation formula
$|G|\sum_{x \in H} f(x) = |H|\sum_{\psi \in H^{\perp}} \hat f(\psi)$. Specializing to
$G = \Z/n$ recovers the classical DFT matrix $e^{-2\pi i kx/n}$ with all of the above.

We then apply the machinery to additive combinatorics. Writing $r_{A,B}(c)$ for the number of
representations $c = a+b$ with $a \in A$, $b \in B$, we prove the Fourier counting formula
$|G|\,r_{A,B}(c) = \sum_{\psi}\psi(c)\widehat{1_{A}}(\psi)\widehat{1_{B}}(\psi)$ and deduce, via
Parseval and Cauchy--Schwarz, that $A+B = G$ whenever $(|G|-|A|)(|G|-|B|) < |A||B|$. We then prove
that this analytic hypothesis is \emph{exactly equivalent} to the pigeonhole hypothesis
$|A|+|B| > |G|$: Cauchy--Schwarz applied to the full nonprincipal spectrum is tight at, and only
at, the pigeonhole threshold. This delimits precisely what cardinality information alone can
achieve. Below the threshold we prove the exact additive-energy identity
$|G|\sum_{c} r_{A,B}(c)^{2} = (|A||B|)^{2} + E$, with
$E = \sum_{\psi \neq 0}|\widehat{1_{A}}(\psi)|^{2}|\widehat{1_{B}}(\psi)|^{2}$, and the resulting
quantitative covering bound $|A+B| \ge |G|(|A||B|)^{2}/((|A||B|)^{2}+E)$, which is attained in
explicit examples.
\end{abstract}

\noindent\textbf{Keywords:} finite abelian group, character, discrete Fourier transform,
Pontryagin duality, Plancherel, convolution theorem, Donoho--Stark uncertainty principle,
additive energy, sumset, group algebra.

\section{Introduction}

Fourier analysis on a finite abelian group is the special case of harmonic analysis in which every
technical difficulty of the continuous theory --- convergence, measurability, integrability,
distributions --- evaporates, leaving only the algebra. Sums are finite, all functions are
integrable, all series converge, and the transform is a linear isomorphism between two
finite-dimensional spaces of the same dimension. What survives is exactly the conceptual content:
\emph{translation-invariant operations are diagonalized by characters.}

This paper is a self-contained development of that theory, pursued far enough to reach two
nontrivial destinations.

The first is the \emph{Donoho--Stark uncertainty principle}: for $f \neq 0$ on a finite abelian
group $G$,
\[
  |\supp f| \cdot |\supp \hat f| \;\ge\; |G|.
\]
Unlike its continuous cousin, this is a purely combinatorial inequality with no constants; it is
sharp, and its equality cases have a rigid algebraic structure that we determine in part.

The second is a \emph{Fourier-analytic sumset theorem}, together with a precise accounting of what
the Fourier method buys over elementary counting. We prove that Cauchy--Schwarz applied to the full
nonprincipal spectrum reproduces the pigeonhole threshold $|A|+|B|>|G|$ \emph{exactly} --- neither
more nor less --- and we identify the exact identity (an additive-energy formula) that must be used
instead if one wants information below that threshold.

Throughout, $G$ denotes a finite abelian group written additively, $|G|$ its order, and all
functions take values in $\C$. The conjugate of $z$ is written $\bar z$.

\section{Characters and the dual group}

\begin{definition}[Character]
A \emph{character} of $G$ is a map $\psi : G \to \C^{\times}$ with $\psi(x+y) = \psi(x)\psi(y)$ for
all $x,y \in G$. The set of characters, with pointwise multiplication, is a group $\dualG$, the
\emph{dual group} (or Pontryagin dual) of $G$. We write its identity --- the trivial character
$x \mapsto 1$ --- as $0$, and use additive notation in $\dualG$, so that
$(\psi-\chi)(x) = \psi(x)\overline{\chi(x)}$.
\end{definition}

Since $G$ is finite, $|G| \cdot x = 0$ for every $x$, hence $\psi(x)^{|G|} = 1$ and
\begin{equation}\label{eq:unimodular}
  |\psi(x)| = 1, \qquad \overline{\psi(x)} = \psi(x)^{-1} = \psi(-x)
  \qquad (x \in G,\ \psi \in \dualG).
\end{equation}

\begin{theorem}[Orthogonality]\label{thm:orth}
For all $x,y \in G$ and all $\psi,\chi \in \dualG$,
\[
  \sum_{\psi \in \dualG} \psi(x)\overline{\psi(y)}
  = \begin{cases} |G|, & x = y,\\ 0, & x \neq y,\end{cases}
  \qquad
  \sum_{x \in G} \psi(x)\overline{\chi(x)}
  = \begin{cases} |G|, & \psi = \chi,\\ 0, & \psi \neq \chi.\end{cases}
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
By \eqref{eq:unimodular}, $\psi(x)\overline{\psi(y)} = \psi(x-y)$, so the first identity reduces to
$\sum_{\psi}\psi(z) = |G|\,[z=0]$; likewise $\psi(x)\overline{\chi(x)} = (\psi-\chi)(x)$ reduces the
second to $\sum_{x}\eta(x) = |G|\,[\eta = 0]$. Both special cases follow from the translation trick:
if $\eta \neq 0$, choose $x_{0}$ with $\eta(x_{0}) \neq 1$; then
$\eta(x_{0})\sum_{x}\eta(x) = \sum_{x}\eta(x_{0}+x) = \sum_{x}\eta(x)$, so
$(\eta(x_{0})-1)\sum_{x}\eta(x) = 0$ and the sum vanishes. Dually for characters.
\end{proof}

An immediate consequence of the second relation is that the characters are linearly independent as
functions on $G$, so $|\dualG| \le |G|$; the inversion theorem below forces equality.

\section{The transform and its fundamental identities}

\begin{definition}
For $f : G \to \C$, the \emph{discrete Fourier transform} is $\hat f : \dualG \to \C$,
\[
  \hat f(\psi) = \sum_{x \in G} \overline{\psi(x)}\, f(x).
\]
The \emph{inverse transform} of $F : \dualG \to \C$ is
$\check F(x) = |G|^{-1}\sum_{\psi \in \dualG} \psi(x) F(\psi)$. The \emph{convolution} of $f,g$ is
$(f*g)(x) = \sum_{y \in G} f(y) g(x-y)$, and the \emph{support} of $f$ is
$\supp f = \{x \in G : f(x) \neq 0\}$.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Fourier inversion]\label{thm:inv}
For every $f : G \to \C$ and every $x \in G$,
$\displaystyle f(x) = \frac{1}{|G|}\sum_{\psi \in \dualG}\psi(x)\hat f(\psi).$
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Expand $\hat f$ and exchange the order of summation:
$\sum_{\psi}\psi(x)\hat f(\psi) = \sum_{y}\bigl(\sum_{\psi}\psi(x)\overline{\psi(y)}\bigr)f(y)
= |G| f(x)$ by Theorem~\ref{thm:orth}, which kills every term except $y = x$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[The transform is a linear isomorphism]\label{thm:iso}
The map $\F : f \mapsto \hat f$ is $\C$-linear and bijective from $\{f : G \to \C\}$ onto
$\{F : \dualG \to \C\}$, with two-sided inverse $F \mapsto \check F$. In particular
$|\dualG| = |G|$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Linearity is immediate. Theorem~\ref{thm:inv} says $(\F f)^{\vee} = f$, giving injectivity; the
dual computation, using the second orthogonality relation in place of the first, gives
$\F \check F = F$, hence surjectivity. The two function spaces are then isomorphic, so
$|\dualG| = |G|$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Parseval--Plancherel]\label{thm:parseval}
For all $f,g : G \to \C$,
\[
  \sum_{\psi \in \dualG} \hat f(\psi)\overline{\hat g(\psi)}
    = |G| \sum_{x \in G} f(x)\overline{g(x)},
  \qquad\text{in particular}\qquad
  \sum_{\psi \in \dualG} |\hat f(\psi)|^{2} = |G| \sum_{x \in G} |f(x)|^{2}.
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Expand both transforms as double sums over $G \times G$, exchange the order of summation so that
the character sum is innermost, and apply Theorem~\ref{thm:orth}, which collapses the double sum to
the diagonal with the factor $|G|$. The norm form is the case $g = f$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Convolution theorem]\label{thm:conv}
For all $f,g$ and all $\psi \in \dualG$, $\ \widehat{f*g}(\psi) = \hat f(\psi)\hat g(\psi)$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
$\widehat{f*g}(\psi) = \sum_{x}\sum_{y}\overline{\psi(x)}f(y)g(x-y)$. Substituting $x = z+y$
(a bijection of $G$ for each fixed $y$) and factoring
$\overline{\psi(z+y)} = \overline{\psi(z)}\,\overline{\psi(y)}$ splits the double sum into the
product $\bigl(\sum_{y}\overline{\psi(y)}f(y)\bigr)\bigl(\sum_{z}\overline{\psi(z)}g(z)\bigr)$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[The square of the transform]\label{thm:square}
Let $\iota : G \to \widehat{\dualG}$ be the canonical embedding $\iota(x)(\psi) = \psi(x)$. Then
$\hat{\hat f}(\iota(x)) = |G| \cdot f(-x)$ for every $f$ and every $x \in G$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
$\hat{\hat f}(\iota(x)) = \sum_{\psi}\overline{\psi(x)}\sum_{y}\overline{\psi(y)}f(y)
= \sum_{y}\bigl(\sum_{\psi}\overline{\psi(x+y)}\bigr)f(y)$, and the inner character sum is $|G|$ if
$x+y=0$ and $0$ otherwise.
\end{proof}

Thus $\F^{2} = |G|\,\mathcal{R}$ with $\mathcal{R}f = f(-\,\cdot\,)$, and since
$\mathcal{R}^{2} = \mathrm{id}$, the normalized transform $|G|^{-1/2}\F$ has order dividing $4$ ---
the finite counterpart of the classical fact that the Fourier transform on $\R$ has eigenvalues
among the fourth roots of unity.

\subsection{The cyclic case: the classical DFT}

\begin{theorem}\label{thm:cyclic}
Let $n \ge 1$ and $G = \Z/n$. For each $k \in \Z/n$ the map $\psi_{k}(x) = e^{2\pi i kx/n}$ is a
character, the assignment $k \mapsto \psi_{k}$ is a group isomorphism
$\Z/n \to \widehat{\Z/n}$, and consequently the abstract transform coincides with the classical DFT
$\hat f(k) = \sum_{x} e^{-2\pi i kx/n}f(x)$. Under this identification, Theorems~\ref{thm:inv},
\ref{thm:parseval} and \ref{thm:conv} read
\[
  f(x) = \frac1n\sum_{k} e^{2\pi i kx/n}\hat f(k),\qquad
  \sum_{k}|\hat f(k)|^{2} = n\sum_{x}|f(x)|^{2},\qquad
  \widehat{f*g}(k) = \hat f(k)\hat g(k),
\]
and Theorem~\ref{thm:uncertainty} below reads $|\supp f|\cdot|\supp \hat f| \ge n$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
That each $\psi_{k}$ is a well-defined character is a direct computation with roots of unity;
injectivity of $k \mapsto \psi_{k}$ follows from $\psi_{k}(1) = e^{2\pi i k/n}$ determining $k$, and
surjectivity from $|\dualG| = |G| = n$. Every abstract statement transfers by relabelling the index
set of the dual along this isomorphism, which changes no sum.
\end{proof}

\section{The transform as the Wedderburn decomposition of $\C[G]$}

The group algebra $\C[G]$ is the vector space of functions $G \to \C$ with convolution as product
and $\delta_{0}$ as unit.

\begin{theorem}[Wedderburn decomposition, abelian case]\label{thm:wedderburn}
The evaluation map
\[
  \mathcal{E} : \C[G] \longrightarrow \C^{\dualG}, \qquad
  \mathcal{E}(a)(\psi) = \sum_{x \in G} a(x)\psi(x),
\]
is an isomorphism of $\C$-algebras, where $\C^{\dualG}$ carries pointwise multiplication. It is the
Fourier transform read at the inverse character: $\mathcal{E}(a)(\psi) = \hat a(-\psi)$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Multiplicativity is the universal property of the group algebra applied to the monoid homomorphism
$x \mapsto (\psi \mapsto \psi(x))$ --- equivalently, it is Theorem~\ref{thm:conv}. The identity
$\mathcal{E}(a)(\psi) = \hat a(-\psi)$ follows from $\overline{(-\psi)(x)} = \psi(x)$. Bijectivity
is Theorem~\ref{thm:iso} composed with the bijection $\psi \mapsto -\psi$ of $\dualG$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}\label{cor:reduced}
$\C[G]$ is reduced: it has no nonzero nilpotent elements.
\end{corollary}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
$\C^{\dualG} \cong \C^{|G|}$ is a finite product of fields, hence reduced; nilpotency is preserved
by algebra isomorphisms in both directions.
\end{proof}

In representation-theoretic language: every complex irreducible representation of a finite abelian
group is one-dimensional, there are exactly $|G|$ of them, and $\C[G] \cong \prod_{\psi}\C$ is the
corresponding decomposition. The Fourier transform \emph{is} that decomposition.

\section{The uncertainty principle}

\begin{theorem}[Donoho--Stark]\label{thm:uncertainty}
Let $G$ be a finite abelian group and $f : G \to \C$ with $f \neq 0$. Then
\[
  |\supp f| \cdot |\supp \hat f| \;\ge\; |G|.
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Put $S = \supp f$, $\hat S = \supp \hat f$ and $M = \max_{x}|f(x)| > 0$, and fix $m$ with
$|f(m)| = M$.

\emph{(i) Fourier coefficients are bounded by $|S|M$.} Only $x \in S$ contribute to
$\hat f(\psi) = \sum_{x}\overline{\psi(x)}f(x)$, and $|\overline{\psi(x)}| = 1$, so
$|\hat f(\psi)| \le \sum_{x \in S}|f(x)| \le |S| M$ for every $\psi$.

\emph{(ii) Inversion at the maximizer.} Only $\psi \in \hat S$ contribute to the inversion formula,
so
\[
  M = |f(m)| = \frac{1}{|G|}\Bigl|\sum_{\psi \in \hat S}\psi(m)\hat f(\psi)\Bigr|
  \le \frac{1}{|G|}\sum_{\psi \in \hat S}|\hat f(\psi)|
  \le \frac{|\hat S|\cdot|S|\,M}{|G|}.
\]

\emph{(iii)} Multiplying by $|G| > 0$ and cancelling $M > 0$ gives $|G| \le |S|\cdot|\hat S|$.
\end{proof}

The proof uses exactly two inequalities, both triangle inequalities, and both are equalities
precisely when the relevant terms are nonnegative multiples of one another. This drives the
extremal analysis of Section~\ref{sec:subgroups} and the open problems of
Section~\ref{sec:discussion}.

\begin{proposition}[Sharpness: Dirac deltas]
Let $\delta_{a}(x) = [x=a]$. Then $\widehat{\delta_{a}}(\psi) = \overline{\psi(a)}$, which is
unimodular and hence never zero, so
$|\supp \delta_{a}| \cdot |\supp \widehat{\delta_{a}}| = 1 \cdot |G| = |G|$.
\end{proposition}

\section{Subgroups, annihilators, and extremals}\label{sec:subgroups}

Throughout this section $H \le G$ is a subgroup, $1_{H}$ its indicator, and
$H^{\perp} = \{\psi \in \dualG : \psi(x) = 1 \text{ for all } x \in H\}$ its \emph{annihilator}.

\begin{lemma}[Orthogonality over a subgroup]\label{lem:subgroup}
For every $\psi \in \dualG$, $\ \sum_{x \in H}\psi(x) = |H|$ if $\psi \in H^{\perp}$, and $0$
otherwise.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
If $\psi \in H^{\perp}$ every summand is $1$. Otherwise pick $x_{0} \in H$ with
$\psi(x_{0}) \neq 1$; translation by $x_{0}$ permutes $H$, so
$\psi(x_{0})\sum_{x \in H}\psi(x) = \sum_{x \in H}\psi(x_{0}+x) = \sum_{x \in H}\psi(x)$, forcing
the sum to vanish.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Transform of a subgroup indicator]\label{thm:indic}
$\widehat{1_{H}} = |H| \cdot 1_{H^{\perp}}$; in particular $\supp \widehat{1_{H}} = H^{\perp}$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
$\widehat{1_{H}}(\psi) = \sum_{x \in H}\overline{\psi(x)} = \sum_{x \in H}(-\psi)(x)$, and
$-\psi \in H^{\perp} \iff \psi \in H^{\perp}$; apply Lemma~\ref{lem:subgroup}.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Subgroup--annihilator duality, from Plancherel]\label{thm:duality}
$|H| \cdot |H^{\perp}| = |G|$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Apply the norm form of Theorem~\ref{thm:parseval} to $f = 1_{H}$. The right-hand side is
$|G|\sum_{x}|1_{H}(x)|^{2} = |G||H|$. By Theorem~\ref{thm:indic} the left-hand side is
$\sum_{\psi}|\widehat{1_{H}}(\psi)|^{2} = |H^{\perp}| \cdot |H|^{2}$. Equating and cancelling
$|H| > 0$ gives the claim.
\end{proof}

This is the standard duality, usually derived from the identification
$H^{\perp} \cong \widehat{G/H}$; the proof above uses nothing but Plancherel's identity.

\begin{corollary}[Subgroup indicators are extremal]\label{cor:extremal-indic}
$|\supp 1_{H}| \cdot |\supp \widehat{1_{H}}| = |H|\,|H^{\perp}| = |G|$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{theorem}[Poisson summation]\label{thm:poisson}
For every $f : G \to \C$, $\ \displaystyle |G|\sum_{x \in H} f(x) = |H| \sum_{\psi \in H^{\perp}}
\hat f(\psi).$
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Replace each $|G| f(x)$ by $\sum_{\psi}\psi(x)\hat f(\psi)$ (Theorem~\ref{thm:inv}), exchange the
order of summation, and evaluate $\sum_{x \in H}\psi(x)$ with Lemma~\ref{lem:subgroup}; only
$\psi \in H^{\perp}$ survive, each with weight $|H|$.
\end{proof}

\subsection{Symmetries and the extremal family}

\begin{definition}
For $a \in G$ and $\chi \in \dualG$ define the \emph{translation} $T_{a}f(x) = f(x-a)$ and the
\emph{modulation} $M_{\chi}f(x) = \chi(x)f(x)$. Call $f$ \emph{extremal} if
$|\supp f|\cdot|\supp \hat f| = |G|$.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Translation $\leftrightarrow$ modulation]\label{thm:symmetries}
For all $f$, $a \in G$ and $\chi,\psi \in \dualG$,
\[
  \widehat{T_{a}f}(\psi) = \overline{\psi(a)}\,\hat f(\psi), \qquad
  \widehat{M_{\chi}f}(\psi) = \hat f(\psi - \chi).
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
For the first, substitute $x = y+a$ and factor
$\overline{\psi(y+a)} = \overline{\psi(y)}\,\overline{\psi(a)}$. For the second, note
$\overline{(\psi-\chi)(x)} = \overline{\psi(x)}\chi(x)$ and read off the definition.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Invariance of extremality]\label{cor:invariance}
The set of extremal functions is invariant under $f \mapsto cf$ ($c \neq 0$), $f \mapsto T_{a}f$,
and $f \mapsto M_{\chi}f$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Scaling by $c \neq 0$ changes neither support. Translation translates $\supp f$ and, by
Theorem~\ref{thm:symmetries}, leaves $\supp \hat f$ unchanged since $\overline{\psi(a)} \neq 0$.
Modulation leaves $\supp f$ unchanged (characters never vanish) and translates $\supp \hat f$
inside $\dualG$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[A family of extremals: modulated cosets]\label{thm:extremals}
Let $H \le G$ be a subgroup, $a \in G$, $\chi \in \dualG$ and $c \in \C^{\times}$. Then
$f(x) = c\,\chi(x)\,1_{H}(x-a)$ satisfies $|\supp f| \cdot |\supp \hat f| = |G|$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
$f = c\,M_{\chi}T_{a}1_{H}$; combine Corollary~\ref{cor:extremal-indic} with
Corollary~\ref{cor:invariance}.
\end{proof}

Concretely, $\supp f = a+H$ is a coset of $H$, $|f|$ is constant on it, and
$\supp \hat f = \chi + H^{\perp}$ is a coset of the annihilator; the trade-off
$|a+H|\cdot|\chi+H^{\perp}| = |G|$ is exactly Theorem~\ref{thm:duality}. In $\Z/12$, for instance,
$H = \{0,3,6,9\}$ gives $|H| = 4$, $|H^{\perp}| = 3$; $H = \{0,4,8\}$ gives $3$ and $4$;
$H = \{0,6\}$ gives $2$ and $6$.

\section{Sumsets: the Fourier counting method and its exact limit}

Fix $A,B \subseteq G$ and define the \emph{representation function}
$r_{A,B}(c) = \#\{(a,b) \in A \times B : a+b = c\}$.

\begin{lemma}\label{lem:rep}
$r_{A,B} = 1_{A} * 1_{B}$, and $\widehat{1_{A}}(0) = |A|$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
$(1_{A}*1_{B})(c) = \sum_{y}1_{A}(y)1_{B}(c-y)$ counts the $y \in A$ with $c-y \in B$, which is the
number of representations. The second claim is immediate from $\overline{0(x)} = 1$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Fourier counting formula]\label{thm:counting}
For every $c \in G$,
\[
  |G| \cdot r_{A,B}(c)
  = \sum_{\psi \in \dualG}\psi(c)\widehat{1_{A}}(\psi)\widehat{1_{B}}(\psi)
  = |A||B| + \sum_{\psi \neq 0}\psi(c)\widehat{1_{A}}(\psi)\widehat{1_{B}}(\psi).
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
By Lemma~\ref{lem:rep} and Theorem~\ref{thm:inv},
$r_{A,B}(c) = |G|^{-1}\sum_{\psi}\psi(c)\widehat{1_{A}*1_{B}}(\psi)$, and Theorem~\ref{thm:conv}
replaces $\widehat{1_{A}*1_{B}}$ by $\widehat{1_{A}}\widehat{1_{B}}$. Splitting off $\psi = 0$ and
using $\widehat{1_{A}}(0)\widehat{1_{B}}(0) = |A||B|$ gives the second form.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[Nonprincipal Parseval mass]\label{lem:mass}
$\displaystyle \sum_{\psi \neq 0}|\widehat{1_{A}}(\psi)|^{2} = |G||A| - |A|^{2}
= |A|\,(|G|-|A|).$
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
Theorem~\ref{thm:parseval} for $f = 1_{A}$ gives $\sum_{\psi}|\widehat{1_{A}}(\psi)|^{2} = |G||A|$;
subtract the term $|\widehat{1_{A}}(0)|^{2} = |A|^{2}$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Cauchy--Schwarz bound on the error]\label{thm:cs}
For every $c \in G$,
\[
  \Bigl|\sum_{\psi \neq 0}\psi(c)\widehat{1_{A}}(\psi)\widehat{1_{B}}(\psi)\Bigr|^{2}
  \;\le\; |A|(|G|-|A|)\cdot|B|(|G|-|B|).
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
By the triangle inequality and $|\psi(c)| = 1$, the left side is at most
$\bigl(\sum_{\psi\neq0}|\widehat{1_{A}}(\psi)||\widehat{1_{B}}(\psi)|\bigr)^{2}$, which by
Cauchy--Schwarz is at most
$\bigl(\sum_{\psi\neq0}|\widehat{1_{A}}(\psi)|^{2}\bigr)
\bigl(\sum_{\psi\neq0}|\widehat{1_{B}}(\psi)|^{2}\bigr)$. Apply Lemma~\ref{lem:mass} twice.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Fourier-analytic sumset theorem]\label{thm:sumset}
If
\begin{equation}\label{eq:cscond}
  (|G|-|A|)(|G|-|B|) \;<\; |A|\,|B|,
\end{equation}
then every $c \in G$ has a representation $c = a+b$ with $a \in A$, $b \in B$; that is, $A+B = G$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Condition \eqref{eq:cscond} forces $|A|,|B| > 0$. Multiplying \eqref{eq:cscond} by $|A||B| > 0$ and
using $|A|(|G|-|A|)\cdot|B|(|G|-|B|) = |A||B|\,(|G|-|A|)(|G|-|B|) < (|A||B|)^{2}$,
Theorem~\ref{thm:cs} gives
$\bigl|\sum_{\psi\neq0}\psi(c)\widehat{1_{A}}(\psi)\widehat{1_{B}}(\psi)\bigr| < |A||B|$. By
Theorem~\ref{thm:counting}, $|G| r_{A,B}(c)$ differs from $|A||B|$ by less than $|A||B|$, hence
$r_{A,B}(c) \ge 1$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[The threshold is exactly pigeonhole]\label{thm:threshold}
For real $N > 0$ and $a,b \in \R$, $\ (N-a)(N-b) < ab \iff N < a+b$. Consequently hypothesis
\eqref{eq:cscond} is equivalent to $|A|+|B| > |G|$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
$(N-a)(N-b) - ab = N^{2} - N(a+b) = N(N-a-b)$, which is negative if and only if $N < a+b$, since
$N > 0$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Pigeonhole]
If $|A|+|B| > |G|$ then $A+B = G$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{remark}
Theorem~\ref{thm:threshold} is the sharpest statement we can make about the \emph{method}: the
Fourier/Cauchy--Schwarz argument recovers the pigeonhole threshold exactly, and does not improve on
it. The reason is structural. Cauchy--Schwarz uses only the total nonprincipal mass
$\sum_{\psi\neq0}|\widehat{1_{A}}(\psi)|^{2} = |A|(|G|-|A|)$, which is determined by $|A|$ alone,
and any argument whose only input is the cardinalities is subject to the extremal examples that
saturate pigeonhole (cosets of a subgroup, for which the entire nonprincipal mass concentrates on
the annihilator). To beat the threshold one must assume more, and the natural assumption is
spectral: a linear uniformity bound $\max_{\psi \neq 0}|\widehat{1_{A}}(\psi)| \le \varepsilon|G|$
reduces the error to $\varepsilon|G|\sqrt{|B|(|G|-|B|)}$ by pulling one factor out of the sum
before applying Cauchy--Schwarz. This is the standard mechanism by which Fourier analysis proves
theorems that counting cannot, and Theorem~\ref{thm:threshold} pinpoints why the cardinality-only
version cannot.
\end{remark}

\section{Below the threshold: energy and covering}

\begin{theorem}[Additive energy identity]\label{thm:energy}
Let $E = \sum_{\psi \neq 0}|\widehat{1_{A}}(\psi)|^{2}|\widehat{1_{B}}(\psi)|^{2} \ge 0$. Then
\[
  |G| \sum_{c \in G} r_{A,B}(c)^{2} \;=\; (|A||B|)^{2} + E .
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Apply the norm form of Theorem~\ref{thm:parseval} to $f = 1_{A}*1_{B}$. On the space side,
$\sum_{c}|(1_{A}*1_{B})(c)|^{2} = \sum_{c} r_{A,B}(c)^{2}$ by Lemma~\ref{lem:rep}. On the dual
side, Theorem~\ref{thm:conv} gives
$\sum_{\psi}|\widehat{1_{A}}(\psi)|^{2}|\widehat{1_{B}}(\psi)|^{2}$; splitting off $\psi = 0$,
whose term is $(|A||B|)^{2}$, and rearranging yields the identity.
\end{proof}

The left-hand sum is the \emph{additive energy} of the pair $(A,B)$: the number of quadruples
$(a,b,a',b')$ with $a+b = a'+b'$. Theorem~\ref{thm:energy} reads it off the spectrum with no loss.

\begin{lemma}[Total mass]\label{lem:total}
$\sum_{c \in G} r_{A,B}(c) = |A||B|$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Exchange the order of summation: $\sum_{c}\sum_{y \in A}[c-y \in B]
= \sum_{y \in A}\#\{c : c-y \in B\} = |A||B|$, since $c \mapsto c-y$ is a bijection of $G$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Quantitative covering bound]\label{thm:covering}
Let $A,B$ be nonempty. Then
\[
  |A+B| \;=\; \#\{c \in G : r_{A,B}(c) > 0\}
  \;\ge\; \frac{|G|\,(|A||B|)^{2}}{(|A||B|)^{2} + E}.
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Let $T = \{c : r_{A,B}(c) > 0\}$. Since $r_{A,B}$ vanishes off $T$, Lemma~\ref{lem:total} gives
$\sum_{c \in T} r_{A,B}(c) = |A||B|$, and Cauchy--Schwarz on $T$ against the constant function $1$
gives
\[
  (|A||B|)^{2} = \Bigl(\sum_{c \in T} r_{A,B}(c)\Bigr)^{2}
  \le |T| \sum_{c \in T} r_{A,B}(c)^{2} = |T| \sum_{c \in G} r_{A,B}(c)^{2}.
\]
Substituting the exact value from Theorem~\ref{thm:energy} and rearranging (the denominator is
positive because $A,B \neq \emptyset$) gives the claim.
\end{proof}

The bound is continuous in the data --- it degrades smoothly as $E$ grows rather than switching off
at a threshold --- and it is attained. In $G = \Z/12$ with $A = \{0,1,2\}$ and $B = \{0,4,8\}$ we
have $|A|+|B| = 6 < 12$, so pigeonhole is silent; but $r_{A,B} \equiv 1$ on nine elements, so
$|G|\sum_{c} r_{A,B}(c)^{2} = 108$ while $(|A||B|)^{2} = 81$, whence $E = 27$ and the bound reads
$|A+B| \ge 12 \cdot 81/108 = 9$, which is exactly $|A+B|$.

\section{Discussion and open problems}\label{sec:discussion}

\subsection{Classification of the uncertainty extremals}

\begin{conjecture}\label{conj:extremals}
For $f \neq 0$ on a finite abelian group $G$, equality $|\supp f|\cdot|\supp \hat f| = |G|$ holds
\emph{if and only if} there exist a subgroup $H \le G$, an element $a \in G$, a character $\chi$
and $c \neq 0$ with $f(x) = c\,\chi(x)\,1_{H}(x-a)$.
\end{conjecture}

Theorem~\ref{thm:extremals} is the ``if'' direction. The ``only if'' direction should follow from
tracking equality in the proof of Theorem~\ref{thm:uncertainty}: equality in step (i) forces $|f|$
to be constant on its support; equality in step (ii) forces the phases $\psi(m)\hat f(\psi)$ to
align for all $\psi \in \supp \hat f$, and hence forces $\overline{\psi(x)}f(x)$ to be independent
of $x \in \supp f$ for each such $\psi$. Phase alignment across a full family of characters is
exactly the assertion that the support is a coset of a subgroup. By
Corollary~\ref{cor:invariance} one may normalize to $f \ge 0$, $0 \in \supp f$, $f(0) = 1$,
reducing the conjecture to a finite combinatorial statement.

\subsection{Tao's uncertainty principle for prime order}

\begin{conjecture}\label{conj:tao}
For $p$ prime and $0 \neq f : \Z/p \to \C$, $\ |\supp f| + |\supp \hat f| \ge p+1$, and this is
sharp for every admissible pair of support sizes.
\end{conjecture}

This is strictly stronger than Theorem~\ref{thm:uncertainty} in the prime case: a hypothetical $f$
with $|\supp f| = |\supp \hat f| = \sqrt p$ would satisfy the product bound and violate the sum
bound. The natural route is Chebotarev's theorem: for $p$ prime, every square submatrix of the
$p \times p$ DFT matrix $\bigl(e^{-2\pi i kx/p}\bigr)$ is nonsingular, which follows from a
resultant/Vandermonde computation in $\Q(\zeta_{p})$ using the irreducibility of the $p$-th
cyclotomic polynomial. Given that, the conjecture is immediate: if $|\supp f| = s$ and
$|\supp \hat f| = t$ with $s+t \le p$, the $t \times s$ submatrix of the transform indexed by the
two supports is nonsingular and yet annihilates a nonzero vector, a contradiction. The failure for
composite orders is explained by Theorem~\ref{thm:extremals}: a proper nontrivial subgroup $H$ has
$|H| + |H^{\perp}| \le |G|$, and such $H$ exist precisely when $|G|$ is composite.

\subsection{Quantitative stability}

\begin{conjecture}\label{conj:stability}
There is an absolute constant $\kappa > 0$ such that for every finite abelian $G$ and every
$0 \neq f : G \to \C$,
\[
  |\supp f| \cdot |\supp \hat f| \;\ge\; |G|\bigl(1 + \kappa\,\delta(f)\bigr),
  \qquad
  \delta(f) = 1 - \frac{\|f\|_{2}^{2}}{\max_{x}|f(x)|^{2} \cdot |\supp f|} \in [0,1),
\]
where $\delta(f)$ measures the deviation of $|f|$ from being constant on its support, so that
$\delta(f) = 0$ exactly for the conjectural extremals of Conjecture~\ref{conj:extremals}.
\end{conjecture}

The only lossy step in the proof of Theorem~\ref{thm:uncertainty} is the pointwise bound
$|f(x)| \le M$; a function whose modulus is far from constant on its support loses a definite
amount there, and the conjecture asserts that this loss can be quantified uniformly.

\subsection{Beyond cardinality, and beyond commutativity}

Theorem~\ref{thm:threshold} shows that cardinality-only hypotheses cannot beat pigeonhole with this
method. The natural next step is the same counting formula under a linear uniformity hypothesis
$\max_{\psi \neq 0}|\widehat{1_{A}}(\psi)| \le \varepsilon|G|$, giving $A+B = G$ under a condition
of the shape $\varepsilon|G|\sqrt{|B|(|G|-|B|)} < |A||B|$, far weaker than $|A|+|B|>|G|$ when $A$
is pseudorandom of density bounded away from $0$; combined with Theorem~\ref{thm:energy} this
should yield a Balog--Szemer\'edi-type statement.

For non-abelian $G$ the characters are replaced by irreducible representations of dimension
$d_{\rho} > 1$ and $\C[G] \cong \bigoplus_{\rho} M_{d_{\rho}}(\C)$. Inversion, Plancherel and the
convolution theorem survive with matrix-valued transforms and traces; the uncertainty principle
survives in a weaker rank form, and the classification of its extremals is entirely open.
Reducedness (Corollary~\ref{cor:reduced}) genuinely fails, since matrix algebras have nilpotents
--- a sharp indication of how much commutativity buys.

\section{Conclusion}

The finite abelian theory is small enough to be proved in its entirety from two orthogonality
relations, and rich enough to contain the fast Fourier transform, Pontryagin duality, Poisson
summation, the Wedderburn decomposition of the group algebra, a sharp uncertainty principle with a
rigid extremal family, and the opening moves of additive combinatorics. Two results deserve
emphasis. First, the subgroup duality $|H| \cdot |H^{\perp}| = |G|$ falls directly out of
Plancherel's identity applied to a subgroup indicator, with no appeal to quotient groups --- a
reminder that duality and energy conservation are the same statement in different clothes. Second,
the exact equivalence $(|G|-|A|)(|G|-|B|) < |A||B| \iff |A|+|B|>|G|$ shows that the
Fourier/Cauchy--Schwarz route to sumset covering is neither weaker nor stronger than pigeonhole,
and thereby identifies precisely where additional spectral hypotheses must enter.

\end{document}

